WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.937
This example demonstrates the idea of creating and importing your own module.

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:10.452
Inside the code, we first create a string called module_code.

00:00:10.452 --> 00:00:15.982
This string contains the code that will go into a file named calculations.py.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:20.267
The module contains two functions.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:24.457
The first function, calculate_profit, subtracts costs from sales.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:31.692
The second function, profit_margin, uses calculate_profit and then divides the profit by sales.

00:00:31.692 --> 00:00:39.262
Next, the code opens a file called calculations.py and writes the module code into that file.

00:00:39.262 --> 00:00:44.537
Then we import calculations just like we would import any other module.

00:00:44.537 --> 00:00:45.862
We also use importlib.reload.

00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:54.112
In a notebook or browser environment, reload helps make sure Python uses the most recent version of the

00:00:54.112 --> 00:00:58.602
file we just created. Then we set sales to 1500 and costs to 1100.

00:00:58.602 --> 00:01:03.837
We call calculations.calculate_profit and calculations.profit_margin.

00:01:03.837 --> 00:01:10.502
This example is important because it shows how a real project can separate helper formulas from the main

00:01:10.502 --> 00:01:15.737
analysis. After running the code, try changing sales and costs.

00:01:15.737 --> 00:01:21.840
Then run the cell again and compare the profit and profit margin.
